WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.800
Could we go offline because
somebody forgot to renew something?

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.770
Four things with an expiry date can do it.

00:00:08.017 --> 00:00:09.107
First, the domain.

00:00:09.537 --> 00:00:12.227
It is rented, usually a year at a time.

00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:14.502
Renewal fails when a card expires,

00:00:15.152 --> 00:00:18.122
or the contact mailbox
belongs to someone who left.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:22.797
Then a stranger can register the name,
and our email arrives at them.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:24.940
Second, DNS.

00:00:25.405 --> 00:00:27.435
One provider answers for everything,

00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:31.675
so if it stops, website,
email and logins stop together.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.590
Signatures expire too.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.810
On 5 May 2026,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.660
a faulty key rollover at the German
registry left most German domains

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.230
unreachable for about three hours.

00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:45.527
Third, a certificate.

00:00:46.052 --> 00:00:48.462
A TLS certificate has an end date.

00:00:48.742 --> 00:00:49.552
When it passes,

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:53.352
browsers warn,
and systems refuse to talk to each other.

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:54.642
In 2018,

00:00:54.792 --> 00:01:00.052
one expired certificate in Ericsson
software took mobile networks offline in

00:01:00.052 --> 00:01:02.342
several countries for most of a day.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:05.977
The internal ones nobody
watches expire on a Sunday.

00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:08.933
Fourth, shorter lifetimes.

00:01:09.123 --> 00:01:12.933
An industry rule, not a law,
cuts how long certificates last.

00:01:13.358 --> 00:01:17.048
Public certificates used to last 398 days.

00:01:17.418 --> 00:01:19.328
From 15 March 2029,

00:01:19.698 --> 00:01:21.868
the maximum is 47 days.

00:01:22.223 --> 00:01:24.333
Renewing by hand will not keep up.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.560
What good looks like.

00:01:26.845 --> 00:01:31.035
One inventory of everything with a date,
each with an owner by name.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.030
Auto-renew on a company account,
with a lock on the main domain.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.800
Alerts thirty days ahead, sent to a team.

00:01:38.135 --> 00:01:40.425
ACME renews certificates by itself.

00:01:40.735 --> 00:01:44.685
NCSC UK guidance suggests auto-renewal,

00:01:45.155 --> 00:01:46.545
a shared contact mailbox

00:01:47.015 --> 00:01:50.085
and two-step verification
on the registrar account.

00:01:51.897 --> 00:01:53.107
A suggested first step.

00:01:53.392 --> 00:01:56.682
Make one list of every domain
and certificate we own.

00:01:56.942 --> 00:01:59.272
Give each an expiry date and an owner.

00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:03.457
Then ask what would stop if the
certificate on our busiest system link

00:02:03.457 --> 00:02:04.887
expired tonight.

00:02:06.517 --> 00:02:07.827
Nobody hacked anything.

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:09.277
A date passed.

00:02:09.627 --> 00:02:11.397
A name on a list prevents it.

